1
00:00:00,430 --> 00:00:02,520
Here is one household's spending,

2
00:00:02,651 --> 00:00:03,800
quarter by quarter.

3
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:05,747
Try to predict the next one.

4
00:00:07,003 --> 00:00:09,571
This year's economics prize went to Robert

5
00:00:09,637 --> 00:00:11,173
Hall and Michael Woodford,

6
00:00:11,473 --> 00:00:13,664
for separate pieces of one idea.

7
00:00:14,113 --> 00:00:16,443
Robert Hall turned the question around.

8
00:00:16,717 --> 00:00:19,288
A household that looks
ahead changes spending

9
00:00:19,338 --> 00:00:20,893
only when news arrives.

10
00:00:21,177 --> 00:00:22,824
And news can't be predicted,

11
00:00:22,894 --> 00:00:24,423
so neither can the change.

12
00:00:25,244 --> 00:00:28,674
In 1978 he tested it on US data:

13
00:00:28,934 --> 00:00:30,581
little predicted the change.

14
00:00:30,824 --> 00:00:32,161
It became a benchmark.

15
00:00:32,644 --> 00:00:34,840
Then, with Mishkin, he measured the

16
00:00:34,903 --> 00:00:37,021
exceptions: in their sample,

17
00:00:37,134 --> 00:00:39,546
about a fifth of spending tracked income.

18
00:00:40,383 --> 00:00:41,933
That was Hall's piece.

19
00:00:42,183 --> 00:00:44,536
Woodford's starts with an older machine.

20
00:00:45,015 --> 00:00:48,015
Older models ran on
links between big totals.

21
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:50,988
In the oil crises of
the nineteen seventies,

22
00:00:51,094 --> 00:00:53,035
the committee writes, they broke.

23
00:00:53,709 --> 00:00:56,739
Robert Lucas argued
models needed foundations:

24
00:00:57,043 --> 00:00:58,590
the decisions themselves.

25
00:00:59,910 --> 00:01:01,930
Woodford, with Julio Rotemberg,

26
00:01:02,085 --> 00:01:04,261
building on Taylor, Calvo and others,

27
00:01:04,463 --> 00:01:06,110
put it into three equations.

28
00:01:07,034 --> 00:01:09,455
One. Households look ahead:

29
00:01:09,505 --> 00:01:11,805
they spend more today
when the real rate is

30
00:01:11,852 --> 00:01:14,326
below the natural rate,
which neither speeds

31
00:01:14,376 --> 00:01:15,705
nor slows spending.

32
00:01:16,135 --> 00:01:17,586
Knut Wicksell's idea.

33
00:01:18,435 --> 00:01:20,805
Two. Changing prices is costly,

34
00:01:20,985 --> 00:01:22,867
so firms set them looking ahead.

35
00:01:23,245 --> 00:01:25,868
Inflation rises when output runs above its

36
00:01:25,918 --> 00:01:26,918
natural level:

37
00:01:27,006 --> 00:01:28,043
the output gap.

38
00:01:28,892 --> 00:01:31,186
Three. The central bank follows a rule:

39
00:01:31,292 --> 00:01:33,709
when inflation rises, raise the rate.

40
00:01:34,568 --> 00:01:38,190
Wire them up, and each
part moves the next.

41
00:01:39,444 --> 00:01:40,934
Now it's a laboratory.

42
00:01:41,194 --> 00:01:42,841
Send in a burst of spending:

43
00:01:42,924 --> 00:01:44,474
households want more, now.

44
00:01:45,455 --> 00:01:47,835
The gap opens, inflation rises,

45
00:01:47,885 --> 00:01:49,297
the rule lifts the rate,

46
00:01:49,405 --> 00:01:50,549
and spending cools.

47
00:01:51,715 --> 00:01:53,775
An energy-cost shock hits firms:

48
00:01:54,035 --> 00:01:55,945
inflation up, output down.

49
00:01:56,195 --> 00:01:58,019
The rule still raises the rate.

50
00:01:59,028 --> 00:02:00,028
Turn the rule up:

51
00:02:00,098 --> 00:02:01,768
inflation rises less;

52
00:02:01,907 --> 00:02:02,966
the dip is deeper.

53
00:02:04,103 --> 00:02:07,056
If inflation rises one
point and the rate less

54
00:02:07,105 --> 00:02:09,843
than one point, the
model loses its anchor:

55
00:02:10,153 --> 00:02:11,798
many paths are possible.

56
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:15,000
The wires carry words too.

57
00:02:15,050 --> 00:02:16,590
To people who look ahead,

58
00:02:16,753 --> 00:02:18,150
a promise is news.

59
00:02:18,486 --> 00:02:20,808
A lower rate promised two years out moves

60
00:02:20,858 --> 00:02:21,858
prices now.

61
00:02:23,049 --> 00:02:24,873
Hold the rate still until then,

62
00:02:24,923 --> 00:02:26,389
and in this simple model,

63
00:02:26,439 --> 00:02:27,939
the further out the promise,

64
00:02:27,989 --> 00:02:29,470
the bigger today's effect,

65
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:30,629
implausibly so:

66
00:02:30,919 --> 00:02:32,566
the forward guidance puzzle.

67
00:02:33,819 --> 00:02:35,519
A laboratory, not an oracle:

68
00:02:35,757 --> 00:02:38,195
it leaves out the floor
on rates near zero,

69
00:02:38,245 --> 00:02:40,769
investment, and any real central bank.

70
00:02:41,139 --> 00:02:44,551
Its core: an economy built
from decisions that look ahead.
